  "query_preview": "..On the large scale, an upper low was centered over \nsouth central Idaho, with a trough axis extending southward through \nthe Great Basin into northern Arizona. Broad high pressure aloft was \ncentered from northeastern Mexico into far eastern Texas. The upper \nlow will continue to move east into the high plains early next week, \nwhile the area of high pressure flattens out across northeast Mexico \nand the southern tier of states. A secondary low then moves into the \ncentral California coast late Sunday and shears out as it lifts to \nthe northeast across the Intermountain West early to mid next week. \nThe area of high pressure amplifies to our east mid to late next \nweek. \n\nSatellite layer blended total precipitable water imagery indicates \nPWATs were currently in the 0.60 to 0.80 inch range across southeast \nArizona. Drier air will continue to move into the region today as \nthe upper low and trough axis shift eastward. PWATs will fall into \nthe 0.40 to 0.65 inch range across the entire CWA today and Sunday. \nThis will shut down convection over the weekend.\n\nBy Monday, as the secondary low begins to move northeast through \nCalifornia into Nevada and the high begins to nudge west and \namplify, flow around these two features begins to pull moisture \nnorthward along the Arizona/New Mexico state line and areas \neastward. Although the official forecast still remains dry for \nsoutheast Arizona on Monday, we will have to monitor any convective \noutflow boundaries over Sonora which may push convection northward \nalong the International Border in Cochise County. PWATs increase \nback into the 0.70 to 0.90 inch range Monday.   \n\nBy mid/late next week the upper high amplifies to our east and flow \nturns back to southerly across southeast Arizona, with increasing \namounts of moisture moving back into southeast Arizona. PWAT values \nare back up into the 1.0 to 1.25 inch range across southeast Arizona.\nThe latest POP grids show a chance of showers/thunderstorms east of \nTucson Tuesday, from Tucson eastward Wednesday and across much of \nsoutheast Arizona Thursday and Friday. In addition, there will be \nthe possibility for some tropical influences late next week. It will \ncome down the position/strength of the upper high on how these \nfeatures track. Still a lot of uncertainty this far out.   \n\nOtherwise, high temperatures through Tuesday are near to slightly \nabove normal, with highs in the mid to upper 90s in the Tucson \nmetro, near 100 degrees across the western deserts and in the mid \n80s to lower 90s across the valley locations of Santa Cruz, Cochise, \nGraham and southern Greenlee counties. Highs then lower 2-4 degrees \nmid to late next week as increasing moisture moves back into the \nregion.",
